2. Basic Recursion Problems :

i, Print Name N Times Using Recursion :

// Print Name N times using Recursion :
// 5 => 5 times name should be printed .


import java.util.*;
public class Main{
    
    public static void PrintName(int n){
        
          if(n==0){
          return;
          }
        
        System.out.println("Madhavan");
          n--;
        PrintName(n);
      
    }
    
    
    public static void main(String[] args){
        
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        PrintName(n);    
    }
}
Your idea in plain words

You created a recursive function PrintName(int n) that takes a number n.

The idea: if n = 5, then print "Madhavan" and keep reducing n until the function stops.

You wrote a base condition:

if(n < 0){
    return;
}


So your stopping point is when n goes below 0.
That means:

If you call with n = 5, it will go like → 5 → 4 → 3 → 2 → 1 → 0 → -1 → stop.

Inside the function, you print the name first:

System.out.print("Madhavan");


This means the printing happens before the recursive call.

Then you reduce n by 1:

n--;
PrintName(n);


So each recursive call is with a smaller number.


Another Approch :
import java.util.*; 
public class Main{
    public static void printName(int i , int n){
        if(i==n){
            return;
        }
           System.out.println("Madhavan");
           printName(i+1 , n);
    }
    public static void main(String[] args){
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        printName(0,n);
    }
}

// 0 => madhavan -> false
// 1 => madhavan -> false
// 2 => madhavan -> false

Comparing with your first approach

First approach (PrintName(n)): you start with the number n and count downwards until 0.

This approach (printName(i, n)): you start with 0 and count upwards until n.

Both give the same result, just a different way of thinking:

Count down: Stop when n==0.

Count up: Stop when i==n.
